xample the  land border crossings are currently  only partly open and neighboring  countries  don't let afghans in  except when they have a visa but it  doesn't seem to happen like often so  this test is just yet to come in front  you know we focus a lot on the people  who are trying to leave who are leaving  the country but  the taliban you know it has 40 million  afghans now that it has to govern and it  is facing lots of crises isn't it  humanitarian crises  indeed uh there's a huge problem and  many afghans that i talked to also say  that like their biggest worry is the  unemployment unemployment was already  before like a huge problem but now it  has like increased even more um funds of  the former government have been frozen  and the the taliban have to now deal  with a  um swiftly deteriorating economic  situation and at the time being there's  like no sign  what or how they they will do anything  against it so this will be like one of  the the biggest tests and one of the  biggest thing to look out uh how the  economic situation of afghanistan will  go forward  okay journalists france marty with the  latest tonight from kabul france as  always thank you  well there is mounting concern about the  fate of women under the taliban those  who've staged rallies demanding the  right to work and to go to school have  been met with violence many fear a  return to repressive rule  [Music]  it takes courage to protest openly  against the taliban especially for women  yet these female protesters in mazay  sharif refuse to be silenced  we demand the creation of secure jobs  both for men and women and the right to  participate in politics for women  the right for women to be selected and  elected for leadership positions and the  right to private ownership  the right for women to work in the  judicial and legal sector and the right  to freedom of speech  but the taliban's strict gender rules  have already started to affect everyday  life  female students returning to university  on the first day of lectures after the  summer break  found they'd been separated from their  male classmates  and now this curtain  it's horrible but we have no choice  it's absurd everything has stopped for  us many people have left i feel lonely  and desperate  when the taliban ruled afghanistan 20  years ago women's rights were severely  curtailed  women were not allowed to work they were  banned from education and only allowed  in public fully covered and accompanied  by a male chaperone  this time around the hardline islamists  have been keen to portray themselves as  more moderate in their first press  conference after taking power their  spokesman promised women's rights would  be honored within the norms of islamic  law  though he failed to elaborate on what  exactly this would mean  many women in afghanistan expect to  return to the dark days of the taliban's  first reign  we spoke to a student at the american  university of afghanistan in kabul  you know like when  when someone is  dreaming for for a better life and  working for 21 years to to  to just reach the dream and suddenly in  one night everything just damaged  just imagine  and now i i might still feel like i'm in  a room where  only there is a darkness  no hope for a better future or no hope  for a lot  this is the feeling that i really have  welcome  that feeling of hopelessness is far from  unwarranted two weeks ago the taliban  told working women to stay at home for  security reasons a measure they said was  temporary  but many fear this is just one of many  steps towards a society in which women  will ultimately be deprived of their  fundamental rights and freedoms  and all but erased from public life  well a senior taliban official has said  that women will be forbidden from  playing cricket or possibly any other  sport because their participation and  i'm quoting here is not necessary now  since the taliban assumed power in  afghanistan the country's women's  football team has been disbanded with a  large number of former players evacuated  to australia on wednesday a taliban  spokesman said female participation in  any events that expose their bodies will  be forbidden  qriket australia has responded saying a  men's cricket match between afghanistan  and australia which is scheduled for  november will be cancelled if the  taliban go through with the ban  it's not a surprise because we knew it  when the when when taliban took away the  country that is like  the first instant action for us for me  especially was to  to get our players as as many as  possible get out of the country because  we knew them we know them they have been  always like this and they will never  change of course they are changed  in terms of like how they communicate  with media but  with what with their action and what  they are doing and especially the  announcement that they are made  they have never been changed that they  don't see a woman in in participating in  any um any activities in society they  don't they just want to vanish women um  they don't see women as part of the  population
 

